 - The app is static-hostable (`npm run build` + serve `dist/` from any HTTP server).
 - You can also just grab a current release zip (contents of `dist/`) from releases, but that will only be built with the default features on.
 
+## Scenario authoring and Moodle work
+
+Swarm now has an initial **scenario authoring** layer intended to make classroom simulations easier to reuse in Moodle-oriented teaching workflows.
+
+Current starting point:
+
+- author a scenario title, summary, learning objectives, teacher notes, and checkpoint prompts
+- choose a runtime target of **MakeCode** or **MicroPython**
+- export a **Moodle XML** question bank for markable checkpoint prompts
+- export a **SCORM ZIP** that keeps the scenario metadata, the saved Swarm bundle, and the Moodle question bank together
+- report an **auto-marked score and lesson status back to the LMS gradebook** from the SCORM launch page
+- support a **learner-mode launch** that can auto-load a packaged `.swarm` bundle via URL parameters
+
+Current limitation:
+
+- the SCORM export is still **not yet a full embedded Swarm runtime**
+- SCORM gradebook reporting currently comes from the package's self-contained checkpoint interactions, while the richer manual-marking path still comes from the exported **question-bank XML**
+
+## Learner-mode embedding groundwork
+
+Swarm now has a first step toward a SCORM-embedded learner mode.
+
+Supported launch parameters:
+
+- `?mode=learner` or `?learner=1` — hide most authoring controls
+- `?bundle=relative/path/to/file.swarm` — fetch and auto-load a packaged Swarm bundle on startup
+
+This means a future SCORM package can launch Swarm directly into a scenario-focused mode instead of dropping students into the general editor.
+
 ## Implementation notes
 
 Swarm was built with Copilot, mostly with GPT 5.3 Codex. I try to test fairly rigorously for the use cases under development
